      "19321": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-14T21:36:35Z",
            "text": "do_arith() computes +, -, * and / on two scalars without attributes directly, but sends %% and %/% to R_binary(), the general vector code. Neither operator has a byte code instruction, so compiled code takes that path too: a scalar xi %% 2L takes 34 ns where x == 2 takes 8.\n\nTimings: arith-scalar-mod-idiv-bench.R, attached, prints ns per evaluation. On aarch64 macOS (Apple clang 21, r90888), xi %% 2L goes from 34 to 27 ns, x %% 2 from 39 to 27, and a loop that tests i %% 2L == 0L from 45 to 36 ns per iteration.\n\nProposed patch: attached as arith-scalar-mod-idiv.diff, against r90888. It adds MODOP and IDIVOP to the scalar path for integer, double and mixed operands. The formulas are shared with the vector code: R_integer_mod() and R_integer_idiv(), next to R_integer_plus(), are also called by the loops in integer_binary(), and the three double branches of the scalar path now go through one helper that uses myfmod() and myfloor() as real_binary() does. Values, types and warnings are unchanged; the regression test compares scalar results with the vector code for 841 pairs of values.\n\nmake check-all passes in CI on Linux, macOS and Windows. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/493, with the full measurements.",
